**Proof.**

First, we establish that $f$ is bijective. From condition (i), $f(f(n))=n$ for all integers $n$, so $f$ is its own inverse; in particular $f$ is both injective and surjective.

**Step 1: Derive a recurrence.** Apply $f$ to both sides of condition (ii), $f(f(n+2)+2)=n$:
$$f\big(f(f(n+2)+2)\big)=f(n).$$
By condition (i) with $n$ replaced by $f(n+2)+2$, the left side equals $f(n+2)+2$. Hence
$$f(n+2)+2=f(n),\qquad\text{i.e.,}\qquad f(n+2)=f(n)-2 \quad\text{for all } n. \tag{$\star$}$$

**Step 2: Determine $f$ on even integers.** Iterating $(\star)$ from $n=0$ and using condition (iii), $f(0)=1$:
$$f(2k)=f(0)-2k=1-2k \quad\text{for all } k\in\mathbb{Z}.$$

**Step 3: Determine $f$ on odd integers.** From (i) with $n=0$: $f(f(0))=0$, i.e., $f(1)=0$. Iterating $(\star)$ from $n=1$:
$$f(2k+1)=f(1)-2k=0-2k=-2k=1-(2k+1) \quad\text{for all } k\in\mathbb{Z}.$$

**Step 4: Combine.** Steps 2 and 3 give $f(n)=1-n$ for every integer $n$ (both parities).

**Step 5: Verify.** With $f(n)=1-n$:
- (i) $f(f(n))=f(1-n)=1-(1-n)=n$. ✓
- (ii) $f(f(n+2)+2)=f\big((1-(n+2))+2\big)=f(1-n)=1-(1-n)=n$. ✓
- (iii) $f(0)=1$. ✓

Therefore $f(n)=1-n$ is the unique integer-valued function on $\mathbb{Z}$ satisfying (i), (ii), and (iii). $\blacksquare$

